        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(x,y)=(n-n^2,n^2+n)$; both values are integers.\n\nClear the denominators, eliminate one variable and substitute.",
          "worked": "Since $n\\ne-1,0,1$, clear the denominators:\n$$\\begin{align*}nx+(n-1)y&=n(n-1)\\qquad(1)\\\\(n+1)x+ny&=n(n+1)\\qquad(2).\\end{align*}$$\nSubtract (1) from (2):\n$$\\begin{align*}((n+1)x+ny)-(nx+(n-1)y)&=n(n+1)-n(n-1)\\\\x+y&=2n\\\\y&=2n-x.\\end{align*}$$\nSubstitute into (1):\n$$\\begin{align*}nx+(n-1)(2n-x)&=n(n-1)\\\\x+2n(n-1)&=n(n-1)\\\\x&=-n(n-1)\\\\x&=n-n^2.\\end{align*}$$\nThen\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=2n-(n-n^2)\\\\y&=n^2+n.\\end{align*}$$\nBoth $n-n^2$ and $n^2+n$ are integers whenever $n$ is an integer. Thus the system always has integer solutions for the permitted values of $n$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 140
      }
    ]
